  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Mostly dry weather prevails this week with continued \nseasonable temperatures after a very chilly start this morning. \n\nVery chilly temperatures out there as of 3AM with 32F in Orange, 35F \nin Taunton, and 35F in Willimantic to name a few. This is the result \nof high pressure overhead promoting efficient radiational cooling \nwith calm winds and clear skies. The exception is along the coast \nand in urban centers where, for instance, Boston and Providence are \n49F and 45F respectively. It will be a quick warm-up today as winds \nback out of the SW advecting in warm air and moisture ahead of a \nweak trough that swings through on Thursday. This trough will do a \nfew things. First, it will make for warmer temperatures. This goes \nfor afternoon highs thanks to a warmer airmass (925mb temps 5C \nwarmer than yesterday) as well as overnight lows (modest LLJ keeping \nthe boundary layer from decoupling). Resulting highs will reach into \nthe mid 60s today and the low 70s on Thursday before a trailing cold \nfront brings cooler weather Friday into the weekend. The second \nimpact of this trough will be increasing moisture going into \nThursday which will lead to higher dewpoints (humidity) and \nincreased cloudcover ahead of the cold front. Marginal moisture, \nthough, will mean showers are isolated at best. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...The holiday weekend favors dry weather for the first \nhalf, but chances are increasing that remnants of a tropical system \nbring rain to the region Sunday/Monday. \n\nHigh pressure solidly in control on Saturday will bring a beautiful, \nseasonably cool day with plenty of sun. Attention then turns to the \nlatter half of the holiday weekend. Unfortunately the probability of \nunsettled weather around Sunday/Monday has increased. We are \nmonitoring Tropical Depression Nine in the Gulf which will \neventually make its way north up the Mississippi Valley, potentially \ninteracting with a northern stream trough and being swept over the \nnortheast bringing wet weather sometime Sunday or Monday. Confidence \nhas increased in this outcome as probabilities of at least 0.01\" of \nrain have increased in both the EPS and GEFS ensembles. Certainly \nnothing is set in stone at this point, but it is worth keeping an \neye on how the forecast evolves over the next several days if you \nhave outdoor plans.",
